var definitions = ['<h2>Elle pratique le violon; elle le pratique.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>pratique</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "pratiquer", which means "to practice". It describes the action being carried out by the subject.</div><div><strong>le violon</strong> This means "the violin" in English. It is a masculine singular noun and is the object of the practice in this sentence.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the masculine singular definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. In the second part of the sentence, it is used as a direct object pronoun, replacing "le violon" to avoid repetition.</div><div><strong>pratique</strong> This is repeated as in the first clause and refers to the practice. Since "le" has replaced "le violon", "pratique" still means "she practices" in English, maintaining the same verb conjugation.</div>','<h2>Tu vois Linda; tu la vois.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vois</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "voir", which means "to see". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>Linda;</strong> This is a proper noun, a person’s name, and does not change in French. Here, the subject is looking at Linda.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is a repetition of the second-person singular pronoun, again referring to "you" informally in English. It is the subject of the second clause.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "her" in English. It refers back to Linda, the person being seen, replacing the noun to avoid repetition.</div><div><strong>vois</strong> This is again the present tense form of the verb "voir". It’s repeated to emphasize the action of seeing Linda, now using the direct object pronoun instead of the proper noun.</div>','<h2>Tu aimes Paul; tu l’aimes.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It is the subject of both clauses in the sentence.</div><div><strong>aimes</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to love" or "to like". In this sentence, it indicates that "you" have affection or fondness for Paul.</div><div><strong>Paul</strong> This is a proper noun, a name referring to a person. In the sentence, Paul is the direct object receiving the action of the verb "aimer" in the first clause.</div><div><strong>tu l’</strong> The phrase "tu l’" is a combination of the second-person singular pronoun "tu" and the direct object pronoun "le", contracted to "l’" before a vowel sound. "Le" represents the masculine singular direct object, in this case referring back to Paul.</div><div><strong>aimes</strong> This is again the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer". Here it is used with the direct object pronoun "l’" to say "you love him" or "you like him", referring back to Paul from the first clause.</div>','<h2>Je fabrique une maison; je la fabrique.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fabrique</strong> This is the first-person singular present indicative form of the verb "fabriquer", which means "to make" or "to build". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>une maison</strong> This phrase means "a house" in English. "Une" is a feminine singular indefinite article, and "maison" is a feminine noun meaning "house". Together they form the direct object of the verb, indicating what is being made.</div><div><strong>;</strong> This symbol is a semicolon in English. It is used to link two independent clauses that are closely related in context.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is repeated from the first clause for emphasis. It is again the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is a feminine singular direct object pronoun in French. It replaces the noun phrase "une maison" to avoid repetition and means "it" in this context, referring back to the house being made.</div><div><strong>fabrique</strong> The verb is repeated from the first clause. It remains in the first-person singular present indicative form of the verb "fabriquer". This repetition along with the pronoun emphasizes the action and the continuity of the action.</div>','<h2>Je mange des bonbons; je les mange.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the first clause, indicating who is performing the action.</div><div><strong>mange</strong> This is the first-person singular present indicative tense of the verb "manger", which means "to eat". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>des bonbons</strong> This is a plural indefinite article "des" followed by the noun "bonbons", which means "candies" in English. The article "des" is used before plural nouns when the quantity is not specified.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Again, this is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the second clause, repeating who is performing the action.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is a direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It refers back to the "bonbons" that were mentioned in the first clause, avoiding repetition of the noun.</div><div><strong>mange</strong> This verb is the same as earlier— the first-person singular present indicative tense of "manger", meaning "to eat". It is repeated here to show the continuity of the action from the first clause to the second.</div>','<h2>Il attache ses lacets; il les attache.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>attache</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "attacher", which means "to tie". It describes the action being done by the subject.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "his" in English. It is used to indicate possession and that the shoelaces belong to the subject.</div><div><strong>lacets</strong> This is a French noun meaning "laces". It’s the direct object of the verb "attache", referring to what is being tied.</div><div><strong>il</strong> This is repeated, and again, it is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject in the second part of the compound sentence.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It replaces "ses lacets" in this part of the sentence, referring back to the laces mentioned earlier.</div><div><strong>attache</strong> This verb is repeated as well and remains in the third-person singular present tense form of "attacher", meaning "he ties".</div>','<h2>Elle se lave les mains; elle se les lave.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun used with reflexive verbs. It indicates that the subject performs an action on itself and corresponds to "herself" in this context.</div><div><strong>lave</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "laver", which means "to wash". It describes the action being performed by the subject on herself.</div><div><strong>les mains</strong> These are the French words for "the hands". "Les" is the definite article for plural nouns and "mains" is the plural form of "main", which means "hand".</div><div><strong>elle se</strong> Here, "elle" is repeated for emphasis, and "se" is again the reflexive pronoun as described above.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, replacing "les mains" mentioned earlier. It’s used here to avoid repetition of the noun phrase "les mains".</div><div><strong>lave</strong> The verb "laver" is repeated here in conjunction with the direct object pronoun "les", maintaining the same tense and meaning as previously mentioned.</div>','<h2>Je regarde les oiseaux; je les regarde.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>regarde</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "regarder", which means "to watch" or "to look at". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, used here in the plural form and means "the". It precedes the noun "oiseaux" to indicate a specific group of birds.</div><div><strong>oiseaux</strong> This is a French noun in the plural form that means "birds". It is the direct object of the verb "regarde", representing what the subject is looking at.</div><div><strong>; </strong> The semicolon is a punctuation mark used to connect closely related independent clauses.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is repeated as the subject of the second clause, again referring to "I" in English.</div><div><strong>les</strong> Here, "les" is a direct object pronoun in the plural form, replacing "oiseaux" to avoid repetition. It means "them" and refers back to the birds.</div><div><strong>regarde</strong> Again, the verb "regarde" is used here in the same form as the first clause, retaining the meaning "to watch" or "to look at".</div>','<h2>Je parle à Linda; je lui parle.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>parle</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak". It indicates the action performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that can often be translated to "to" in English. It is used here to specify the object of the verb — the person who is being spoken to.</div><div><strong>Linda</strong> This is a proper noun, a person’s name. In the sentence, it is the indirect object of the verb "parle", the person to whom the speech is directed.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Repeated as the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s again the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "to him/her" in English. It replaces "à Linda" to avoid repetition and directly refers to Linda in the second clause.</div><div><strong>parle</strong> Repeated as the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "parler". It shows the continued action of the subject speaking to Linda.</div>','<h2>J’écris à Paul; je lui écris.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je" when followed by a vowel sound. It is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English, and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>écris</strong> This word is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "écrire", which means "to write". It represents the action that the subject (I) am performing.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This French preposition is equivalent to "to" in English. It is used before the object of the verb to indicate the direction of the action towards a person or place.</div><div><strong>Paul</strong> Paul is a proper noun in French; it’s a person’s name. In this sentence, Paul is the indirect object receiving the action of the verb.</div><div><strong>;</strong> The semicolon here connects closely related independent clauses.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s used again as the subject for the second clause.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a French indirect object pronoun, equivalent to "to him" or "to her" in English. It replaces the noun "Paul" to avoid repetition and indicate that the action is directed towards Paul.</div><div><strong>écris</strong> Just as in the first clause, it is the first-person singular of the present tense form of the verb "écrire" with the same meaning "to write".</div>','<h2>Elle lance le bâton à son chien; elle lui lance le bâton.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular female pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lance</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "lancer", which means "to throw" in English. It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>le bâton</strong> This phrase means "the stick" in English. "Le" is the definite article for masculine singular nouns, and "bâton" is a masculine singular noun meaning "stick".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This preposition in French can have several meanings, but in this context, it means "to". It’s used here to indicate the direction of the action towards the dog.</div><div><strong>son chien</strong> This phrase means "her dog" in English. "Son" is a possessive adjective that can mean "his", "her", or "its" in English depending on the context, and "chien" is a masculine singular noun meaning "dog". Here "son" agrees in gender and number with "chien", not the gender of the possessor.</div><div>The semicolon (;) in French is used similarly to English, to link two independent clauses that are closely related in content.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> Again, "elle" is the third-person singular female pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the second clause.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "him" or "her" in English. It’s used before the verb to indicate to whom the action is directed, in this case, to her dog.</div><div><strong>lance</strong> This term is repeated from the first clause and retains the same meaning.</div><div><strong>le bâton</strong> Again, this phrase means "the stick" in English and is repeated from the first clause.</div>','<h2>Il achète des fleurs pour Marie; il lui achète des fleurs.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>achète</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "acheter", which means "to buy". In this sentence, it depicts the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>des fleurs</strong> This phrase means "flowers" in English. The article "des" is used here as a partitive article, indicating an unspecified quantity of flowers.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It shows the beneficiary or purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>Marie</strong> This is a proper noun, a female first name in French. In the sentence, it’s the person for whom the subject is buying flowers.</div><div><strong>il</strong> Once again, this is the third-person singular pronoun, repeated as the subject of the second independent clause.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to her" or "for her" in English. It refers to Marie, indicating that the action of buying is done for her benefit.</div><div><strong>achète</strong> This verb is repeated from the first clause, once again in the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "acheter".</div><div><strong>des fleurs</strong> The phrase for "flowers" is repeated, maintaining the partitive article to indicate an unspecified quantity of flowers being bought.</div>','<h2>J’enseigne aux élèves; je leur enseigne.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. The contraction is used before verbs that begin with a vowel sound to ease pronunciation. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>enseigne</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "enseigner", which means "to teach". The speaker is describing an action they perform.</div><div><strong>aux élèves</strong> This phrase means "to the students". "Aux" is a contraction of the preposition "à" (to) and the definite article "les" (the), and it precedes the plural noun "élèves", which means "students". This indicates the indirect objects of the verb "enseigner", the individuals who receive the action of teaching.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is repeated as the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject again for the second clause.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to them". It replaces "aux élèves" in the second clause to avoid repetition.</div><div><strong>enseigne</strong> The verb "enseigne" is used once more. Here it is connected to "leur", showing that "I teach" is being done to "them" (the students).</div>','<h2>J’ai fait des biscuits pour les enfants; je leur ai fait des biscuits.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je" (I) before a vowel or mute h. It is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English.</div><div><strong>ai fait</strong> This is the first-person singular past tense form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make". It indicates a completed action. In this case, it translates to "have made".</div><div><strong>des biscuits</strong> This phrase translates to "some cookies" or "cookies" in English. "Des" is a plural indefinite article, and "biscuits" is the plural form of "biscuit", which means "cookie".</div><div><strong>pour les enfants</strong> This phrase means "for the children". "Pour" is a preposition that means "for", and "les enfants" is the plural form of "l’enfant", which means "the children".</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the second clause.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is the third-person indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English, when referring to an indirect object. In this case, it means "to them", referring back to the children.</div><div><strong>ai fait</strong> As previously mentioned, this is the first-person singular past tense form of the verb "faire", meaning "have made".</div><div><strong>des biscuits</strong> Again, this phrase translates to "some cookies" or "cookies" in English.</div>','<h2>Elle lit un livre à ses petits-enfants; elle leur lit un livre.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of both clauses in the sentence.</div><div><strong>lit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>un livre</strong> This is a French noun phrase meaning "a book". "Un" is an indefinite article equivalent to "a" in English, and "livre" means "book". It’s the direct object of the verb "lit", indicating what is being read.</div><div><strong>à ses petits-enfants</strong> This phrase indicates to whom the book is being read. "À" means "to", "ses" is a possessive adjective meaning "her" referring to things or people associated with "elle" (since it’s plural here, it means "her" as in "her own"), and "petits-enfants" means "grandchildren".</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This pronoun appears again, still signifying "she" and remaining the subject of the second clause.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to them". It replaces the phrase "à ses petits-enfants" in the second clause for conciseness.</div><div><strong>lit un livre</strong> This part is identical to the first occurrence, with "lit" as the verb "reads" and "un livre" meaning "a book".</div>','<h2>Il explique son projet à ses collègues; il leur explique son projet.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual or a masculine entity.</div><div><strong>explique</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "expliquer", which means "to explain". It indicates that the subject is in the process of explaining something.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, meaning "his" or "her" (or "its" when referring to a noun of neutral gender in English). Here it denotes ownership or association with the noun "projet" that follows.</div><div><strong>projet</strong> This is a masculine noun in French, which translates to "project" in English. It’s the object of the verb "expliquer" and the thing being explained to the colleagues.</div><div><strong>à ses collègues</strong> This phrase means "to his colleagues" in English. The preposition "à" in French is used here to indicate the indirect object of the verb "expliquer", showing who the project is being explained to.</div><div><strong>il leur explique</strong> In this phrase, "il" remains the subject pronoun "he". The word "leur" is an indirect object pronoun that stands for "à ses collègues" (to his colleagues). "Explique" continues to be the third-person singular form of the verb "expliquer".</div><div><strong>son projet</strong> As before, "son" is the possessive adjective "his/her", and "projet" means "project". This phrase repeats the direct object of the verb "expliquer", even though in the structure of the second part of the sentence it is written at the end instead of immediately after the verb, due to the use of the indirect object pronoun "leur". The second part of the sentence is a more concise way to say the first part, using the indirect object pronoun to eliminate repetition.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Nous ne pourrons pas le réparer nous-mêmes.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is a negation particle in French, used alongside "pas" to form a negative construction. When "ne" precedes a verb, it indicates that the action of the verb is negated.</div><div><strong>pourrons</strong> This is the future simple tense of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". In this case, "pourrons" is conjugated to agree with the first-person plural subject "nous", meaning "we will be able to".</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation, which, when used with "ne", negates the verb. In this sentence, it creates the meaning "will not be able to".</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "it" in English, referring to a masculine singular noun that was previously mentioned or is already known.</div><div><strong>réparer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French, meaning "to repair" or "to fix". In this sentence, it is used as the action that the subject cannot perform.</div><div><strong>nous-mêmes</strong> This phrase combines the reflexive pronoun "nous" with "mêmes" to become an emphatic pronoun meaning "ourselves" in English. It emphasizes who was intended to perform the action, in this case indicating that the subjects themselves will not do the repairing.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que tu penses que l’arbre tomberait si tu ne le tenais pas?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a French expression used to introduce a question. It can be translated as "Is it that" and is commonly used in spoken French to form questions.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally.</div><div><strong>penses</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "penser", which means "to think". In this sentence, it indicates what "you" are thinking or considering.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in French that means "that". It is used to connect the verb "penses" to the dependent clause that follows.</div><div><strong>l’arbre</strong> This is a contraction of "le arbre", where "le" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "arbre" means "tree" in French. The contraction is used before a vowel or mute h.</div><div><strong>tomberait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense of the verb "tomber", meaning "would fall". It indicates a hypothetical situation or action that could occur under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It introduces a conditional clause expressing the condition under which the tree would fall.</div><div><strong>tu ne</strong> The word "tu" here again refers to "you" in the second-person singular, while "ne" is a negation particle used in negating verbs. In spoken French, "ne" is frequently dropped, leaving just the second part of the negation.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the third-person singular direct object pronoun "it", referring back to "l’arbre" (the tree).</div><div><strong>tenais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "tenir", which means "to hold". Here it suggests a continuous action in the past or a hypothetical situation that might have been ongoing.</div><div><strong>pas?</strong> "Pas" completes the negation started by "ne", and it translates to "not" in English. The question mark indicates that the sentence is a question.</div>','<h2>Oui. Vous ne pouvez pas la manquer.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It can denote a formal address, or refer to more than one person.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is the negative adverb used in French negation. It precedes the verb and works in tandem with other negative words to negate a sentence.</div><div><strong>pouvez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" in English. Here, it indicates ability or permission in the negative form.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> Along with "ne", it forms a negative structure negating the verb. "Ne... pas" is the standard way to negate a sentence in French, equivalent to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the direct object pronoun referring to a feminine noun in French. It’s replacing the noun that’s being discussed and understood in the conversation.</div><div><strong>manquer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "manquer", which means "to miss" in English. In this context, it could mean to fail to see or to be unable to miss in the sense of something being obvious or easy to find.</div>','<h2>Pourriez-vous la nettoyer aujourd’hui?</h2><div><strong>Pourriez-vous</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". Here, it’s used to form the polite question "could you". It combines the conditional tense of "pouvoir" with the second-person formal or plural "vous", which can also refer to a singular individual in a formal context.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, and it translates to "it" or "her" in English. It’s feminine singular, and it’s used here to refer to a feminine noun that is being alluded to in the conversation.</div><div><strong>nettoyer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to clean" in French. As an infinitive, it acts like a noun or is used after another verb. In this case, it follows "pourriez-vous" to specify what action is being asked about.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is an adverb meaning "today". It specifies the time when the action in question should be performed.</div>','<h2>Je vais la prendre pour six jours.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It indicates who is performing the action in the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it is used to form a near future tense, expressing an action that will take place soon.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular direct object pronoun in French, meaning "her" or "it" in English. In this case, it refers to a feminine noun that has been previously mentioned or is understood from context.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is an infinitive form of the verb meaning "to take". It is used here after the conjugated verb "vais" to create the future proche (near future) tense.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "for" in English. It indicates the duration or purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>six jours</strong> This is a time expression meaning "six days" in English. It indicates the length of time for which the action of taking will last.</div>','<h2>Je n’ai pas le choix, madame, je dois la jeter.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>n’ai pas</strong> This phrase is a combination of "ne" (negative particle, used before the verb), "ai" (the first-person singular present tense form of "avoir", which means "to have"), and "pas" (negative particle, used after the verb). Together, they negate the verb, making the phrase equivalent to "do not have" in English.</div><div><strong>le choix</strong> This means "the choice" in English. Here it’s used as a direct object, referring to what the subject does not have.</div><div><strong>madame</strong> This is a French term of address for a woman, equivalent to "ma’am" or "madam" in English. It shows respect or politeness when addressing a woman.</div><div><strong>je dois</strong> The word "je" again stands for "I". The verb "dois" comes from "devoir", which is an infinitive meaning "to have to" or "must". Here, it’s in the first-person singular present indicative, meaning "I must" or "I have to".</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is a feminine singular definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. Here, it is used before a feminine singular noun as a direct object pronoun, replacing the object being referred to which is not mentioned but understood in context.</div><div><strong>jeter</strong> "Jeter" is a verb in French that means "to throw" in English. In this sentence, it’s in the infinitive form, which is being used after the verb "dois", creating the meaning "I must throw".</div>','<h2>La voiture ne fonctionne plus, donc je la répare.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the French definite article for feminine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify the noun "voiture".</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine noun in French that means "car". It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne ... plus</strong> This is a negation phrase in French. "Ne" precedes a verb while "plus" follows it, together meaning "no longer" or "not ... anymore" in English.</div><div><strong>fonctionne</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "fonctionner", which means "to work" or "to function". In this sentence, it is negated by "ne ... plus".</div><div><strong>donc</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "therefore" or "so". It’s used as a conjunctive adverb to link the cause of the car not working with the action of repairing it.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It serves as the subject of the second clause in the sentence.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "it" when replacing a feminine noun (in this case "la voiture"). It’s used to avoid repetition of the noun "voiture".</div><div><strong>répare</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "réparer", which means "to repair". It describes the action the subject (je) is taking with respect to the car.</div>','<h2>Oui, je voudrais les essayer.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It responds affirmatively to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional tense of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It indicates a polite desire or wish in this context.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the plural definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun it modifies, which is plural.</div><div><strong>essayer</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to try". In this sentence, it’s used following the verb "voudrais", creating the meaning "would like to try".</div>','<h2>Mon mari les a fabriquées lui-même.</h2><div>  <strong>Mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French meaning "my". It is used here to denote possession and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "mari" (husband).</div><div>  <strong>mari</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "husband". It is the subject of the sentence, being the one who performed the action.</div><div>  <strong>les</strong> This is a direct object pronoun in French meaning "them". It replaces the noun that was previously mentioned or is understood by the context, and agrees in number and gender with the noun it represents.</div><div>  <strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the past tense, known as the "passé composé".</div><div>  <strong>fabriquées</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "fabriquer", which means "to make" or "to manufacture". It agrees in gender (feminine) and number (plural) with the direct object pronoun "les", suggesting that the objects referred to are feminine and plural.</div><div>  <strong>lui-même</strong> This is an emphatic pronoun phrase, composed of "lui" (him) and "même" (self), meaning "himself". It emphasizes that the husband performed the action by himself, without help.</div>','<h2>Je crois que je vais les prendre.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the phrases "je crois" and "je vais".</div><div><strong>crois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "croire", which means "to believe" or "to think". In the sentence, it’s used to indicate the speaker’s belief or assumption.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction in French that means "that". It introduces a subordinate clause "que je vais les prendre".</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it is used with an infinitive to form a futur proche, indicating a near future action.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is a direct object pronoun in French that means "them". It replaces the plural noun that the speaker intends to take.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French that means "to take". Here, it’s used in conjunction with "vais" to indicate what the speaker is planning to do, forming the futur proche (near future).</div>','<h2>Je dois les prendre avec de la nourriture?</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It expresses an obligation or a necessity.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It refers back to a previously mentioned or understood plural object.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to take" in French. It is used here in combination with the verb "devoir" (to have to), indicating what the subject must take.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a preposition meaning "with" in English. It introduces a phrase that describes the manner or condition in which something is to be done.</div><div><strong>de la</strong> This is a partitive article in French, equivalent to "some" in English. It is used to express an unspecified quantity of a mass or uncountable noun.</div><div><strong>nourriture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "food". It is a feminine singular noun that represents the thing with which the subject should take "them".</div>','<h2>Super! J’ai hâte de les essayer!</h2><div><strong>Super!</strong> This is a French exclamation meaning "Great!" or "Super!". It expresses enthusiasm or satisfaction.</div><div><strong>J’ai</strong> This comes from the verb "avoir", which means "to have". "J’ai" is the first-person singular present tense, meaning "I have". In French, "avoir" is used in many idiomatic expressions.</div><div><strong>hâte</strong> This is a noun meaning "haste" or "hurry". In the expression "J’ai hâte", it means "I can’t wait" or "I’m looking forward to".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that, in this context, means "to" as part of the verb phrase "hâte de". It is used to link the verb to the infinitive that follows.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article for plural nouns, meaning "the". Here it refers to specific items or experiences that the speaker is excited to try.</div><div><strong>essayer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to try". In the sentence, "J’ai hâte de les essayer", it means the speaker is looking forward to trying them out.</div>','<h2>Merci. Vous les avez dans une autre couleur?</h2><div><strong>Merci</strong> This is the French word for "thank you". It’s an expression of gratitude.</div><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. In this context, it is formal and is also used when speaking to more than one person.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French meaning "them", referring to a previously mentioned or understood object or objects.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". It indicates possession or presence of something.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "in" in English. It’s used here to indicate that something is within something else or to express inclusion.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the French indefinite article, which translates to "a" or "an" in English. It’s feminine singular to agree with the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>autre</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "other" or "another". It suggests an alternative to what has been previously mentioned or an additional option.</div><div><strong>couleur?</strong> This is the French word for "color". As a noun, it refers to the characteristic of visual perception described through color categories. It’s placed at the end of the question to ask about the availability of different color options.</div>','<h2>Assurez-vous de les prendre jusqu’à la fin.</h2><div><strong>Assurez-vous</strong> This is a reflexive imperative form of the verb "assurer", which means "to ensure" or "to make sure". In this case, it’s used in the second-person formal or plural imperative mood, telling the listener to ensure or to make sure of something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition which, in this context, translates to "to" in English. It is used to link the verb "assurez-vous" to the following infinitive verb phrase.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, meaning "them". It’s used here to refer to objects or things that should be taken until the end.</div><div><strong>prendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to take". In this sentence, it’s used after "de", following the structure "assurez-vous de" + infinitive verb, which means "make sure to" + verb.</div><div><strong>jusqu’à</strong> This is a preposition meaning "until". It is used to indicate a time or event at which the action of taking them should continue.</div><div><strong>la fin</strong> This means "the end". It is a noun phrase, denoting the point at which the action of taking should stop.</div>','<h2>Tu sécheras tes vêtements après les avoir lavés.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sécheras</strong> This is the future tense form of the verb "sécher", which means "to dry". The future tense indicates that the action will occur later.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English, referring to something that belongs to the person being spoken to. It is here in its masculine or feminine plural form to match "vêtements" (clothes).</div><div><strong>vêtements</strong> This is a French noun meaning "clothes". It is plural here to refer to multiple items of clothing.</div><div><strong>après</strong> This is a French preposition that means "after". It’s used to indicate the sequence of events.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the direct object pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It refers back to "vêtements" (clothes) that the subject will dry.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is the auxiliary verb used in French to form compound tenses. Here it is used to create the past infinitive "avoir lavés" (to have washed).</div><div><strong>lavés</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "laver", which means "to wash". Combined with "avoir", it’s part of the past infinitive construction which indicates the clothes have been washed before they are to be dried.</div>','<h2>Je ne crois pas qu’ils fuient si tu les laisses libres.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This word is part of the negation in French. It is used before the verb to negate a sentence, generally paired with "pas" after the verb.</div><div><strong>crois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "croire", which means "to believe" or "to think". In this sentence, it’s used in a negative form indicating disbelief.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation in French. Along with "ne", it negates the verb with which it is used, in this case "crois".</div><div><strong>qu’ils</strong> "Que" is a conjunction meaning "that", and "ils" is the third-person plural pronoun, meaning "they". Together, "qu’ils" introduces a subordinate clause meaning "that they".</div><div><strong>f uient</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "fuir", which means "to flee" or "to run away".</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction meaning "if" in English. It introduces a conditional clause.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is the subject of the conditional clause.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is a direct object pronoun meaning "them", referring to some previously mentioned objects or people.</div><div><strong>laisses</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "laisser", which means "to let" or "to leave". It indicates allowing or permitting an action.</div><div><strong>libres</strong> This is the plural form of the adjective "libre", which means "free". It agrees in number with the object it describes, in this case it implies the state of being free or at liberty.</div>','<h2>Je lui donnerai bientôt les clés.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence who will perform the action.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "him" or "her" in English, depending on the context. It represents the person to whom the keys will be given.</div><div><strong>donnerai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "donner", which means "to give". It indicates that the action of giving will happen in the future.</div><div><strong>bientôt</strong> This is an adverb meaning "soon" in English. It gives information about the time when the keys will be given.</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It’s used here to specify the noun that follows it, which is "keys" in English.</div><div><strong>clés</strong> This is a French noun meaning "keys". It’s in the plural form and it’s the object that will be given.</div>','<h2>C’est bien! Et lui as-tu parlé en français?</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This contraction combines the third-person singular pronoun "ce" (it) with the third-person singular of the verb "être" (to be), forming "c’est", which means "it is" or "that’s" in English. It is often used to give an opinion or state a fact.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is an adverb meaning "well" or "good" in English. It’s used to indicate agreement or satisfaction with something.</div><div><strong>Et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a third-person singular indirect object pronoun in French, meaning "to him" or "to her" in English. It indicates to whom the action of the verb is directed.</div><div><strong>as-tu</strong> This is a question formation that uses the second-person singular form of the verb "avoir" (to have), which is "as". The subject pronoun "tu" (you) follows it, forming "as-tu", which means "have you" in English. It’s used here to ask if the action has been done by the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>parlé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" in English. In this question, it’s used to inquire whether the action of speaking has taken place.</div><div><strong>en français</strong> This phrase means "in French" in English. It specifies the language that was potentially used during communication.</div>','<h2>Elle comprend quand nous lui expliquons.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to a female person.</div><div><strong>comprend</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "comprendre", which means "to understand". It demonstrates the action she is performing when the clause that follows occurs.</div><div><strong>quand</strong> This is the French word for "when". It introduces a dependent clause indicating the time at which the action of the main clause (she understands) happens.</div><div><strong>nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the verb in the dependent clause.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French, referring to "her" in English. It indicates to whom the action is being performed in the dependent clause.</div><div><strong>expliquons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "expliquer", which means "to explain". It shows the action that "we" are performing toward "her" whenever the condition "when" is met (in the dependent clause).</div>','<h2>Je suggère que tu lui apportes des fleurs.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating that the speaker is the one performing the action of suggesting.</div><div><strong>suggère</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "suggérer", which means "to suggest". The speaker is recommending an action to another person.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This French word translates to "that" in English. It’s a conjunction here used to introduce the subordinate clause "tu lui apportes des fleurs" which details what the speaker is suggesting.</div><div><strong>tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the subordinate clause and indicates the person being asked to perform the action.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a third-person singular indirect object pronoun, equivalent to "him" or "her" in English. It indicates to whom the action is directed, meaning here that the flowers should be brought to another person (not directly to "you" or "I").</div><div><strong>apportes</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "apporter", which means "to bring". The verb is in the subjunctive mood because it’s expressing a desire or suggestion.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a partitive article in French meaning "some" in English. It’s used here before the noun "fleurs" to indicate an unspecified quantity of flowers.</div><div><strong>fleurs</strong> This is a plural noun in French, meaning "flowers" in English. This is the object of the verb "apportes", indicating what the speaker suggests to bring to someone.</div>','<h2>Elle a souri quand je lui ai montré la photo.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It is the subject of the sentence referring to a female individual.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have" in English. It is used here to form the compound past tense, known as le passé composé, with the next verb "souri".</div><div><strong>souri</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "sourire", which means "to smile" in English. Paired with the auxiliary verb "a", it indicates that the action of smiling happened in the past.</div><div><strong>quand</strong> This is a French conjunction meaning "when". It introduces a clause that specifies the time at which the action occurred.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is a third-person singular indirect object pronoun in French. It means "to him" or "to her" in English and is used here to indicate to whom the action is being done, i.e., "to her" or "to him".</div><div><strong>ai</strong> Similarly to "a", this is the first-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", used to form the compound past tense in the subordinate clause.</div><div><strong>montré</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "montrer", which means "to show" in English. It’s combined with the auxiliary verb "ai" to denote the past action of showing something.</div><div><strong>la photo</strong> This phrase means "the photo" in English. "la" is the feminine singular definite article "the", and "photo" is a shortened form of "photographie", which means "photo or picture". It is the direct object of the verb "montré", indicating what was shown.</div>','<h2>J’ai rencontré une fille très jolie et je voulais lui parler.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je" which is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is contracted with the following verb that starts with a vowel sound to ease pronunciation.</div><div><strong>ai rencontré</strong> This is the past tense form of the verb "rencontrer", which means "to meet". "ai" is the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the first-person singular. Together with the past participle "rencontré", it forms the passé composé, which is used to express a completed action in the past.</div><div><strong>une fille</strong> This translates to "a girl" in English. "une" is the indefinite feminine singular article, and "fille" is the noun for girl.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It is used to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>jolie</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "pretty" or "lovely". It is in the feminine singular form to agree with "une fille".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It is a conjunction used to connect words, phrases, or clauses.</div><div><strong>je</strong> Again, this is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the second independent clause.</div><div><strong>voulais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It describes a continuous state or repeated actions in the past.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is the indirect object pronoun meaning "to him" or "to her" in English. In this case, it refers to "une fille", which is why the feminine indirect object pronoun is used.</div><div><strong>parler</strong> This is the infinitive verb meaning "to talk" or "to speak" in English. It is used in conjunction with the verb "voulais" to express the action the subject wanted to perform.</div>','<h2>Elle ramasserait le sac bleu si son père le lui donnait.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It refers to a female and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ramasserait</strong> This is the conditional tense form of the verb "ramasser", which means "to pick up". The conditional mood is used to express what would happen under certain circumstances.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. Here, it is used before a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>sac bleu</strong> This is a noun phrase meaning "blue bag". "Sac" is a masculine singular noun meaning "bag", and "bleu" is an adjective meaning "blue", which agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It introduces a condition.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "her" in English. It shows ownership, indicating that the father belongs to the female subject mentioned as "elle".</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a noun meaning "father" in English.</div><div><strong>le</strong> Here, "le" is a direct object pronoun meaning "it", replacing the earlier mentioned noun phrase "le sac bleu".</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to her". In this case, it indicates to whom the action is being done, referring contextually to the female subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>donnait</strong> This is the imperfect tense form of the verb "donner", which means "to give". The imperfect indicates an ongoing action or state in the past, often translated as "was giving" or "used to give". In this sentence, it triggers the condition in the si-clause for the conditional "ramasserait".</div>','<h2>Elle lui a donné un baiser sur la joue.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is the third-person singular indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "him" or "her" in English, depending on the context. In this sentence, it refers to the person receiving the action. </div><div><strong>a donné</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense (passé composé) of the verb "donner", which means "to give". The auxiliary verb "a" is a form of "avoir" and "donné" is the past participle of "donner".</div><div><strong>un baiser</strong> This phrase means "a kiss" in English. "Un" is the indefinite article for a masculine noun, and "baiser" is the noun "kiss".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a preposition meaning "on" in English. It indicates the location of the kiss in this context.</div><div><strong>la joue</strong> This means "the cheek" in English. "La" is the definite article for a feminine noun, and "joue" is the noun "cheek".</div>','<h2>J’aimerais bien lui parler, c’est important.</h2><div><strong>J’aimerais</strong> This is the conditional form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". Here, it expresses a polite desire, and when used with "bien", translates to "I would really like". "J’aimerais" is a contraction of "Je aimerais", where "Je" becomes "J’" before a vowel sound.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is an adverb that enhances the verb "aimerais". It translates to "well" or "really" and intensifies the speaker’s wish to speak to someone.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to him" or "to her". In this sentence, it’s used to indicate the person to whom the speaker wishes to speak.</div><div><strong>parler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to speak" or "to talk". In this sentence, it directly follows the pronominal construct "J’aimerais bien lui" to denote what the speaker would like to do.</div><div><strong>c’est</strong> This is a contraction of "cela est", translating to "it is". "C’est" is often used in French for stating facts or emphasizing something.</div><div><strong>important</strong> This is an adjective meaning "important". In this sentence, it qualifies the necessity or significance of the action of speaking to the person mentioned earlier.</div>','<h2>Nous lui enseignerions les sciences, mais il préfère apprendre seul.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence who would perform the action of teaching.</div><div><strong>lui</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French. Placed before the verb, it represents to whom the action is directed. In this case, "him" or "her".</div><div><strong>enseignerions</strong> This is the first-person plural conditional tense form of the verb "enseigner", which means "to teach". The conditional mood is used to express actions that could happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>les sciences</strong> This phrase means "the sciences" in English. It’s a direct object noun phrase consisting of the definite article "les" and the noun "sciences", referring to subjects such as biology, chemistry, physics, etc.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This word translates to "but" in English. It’s a conjunction used to introduce a contrast or an exception to what has been previously stated.</div><div><strong>il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. Here, it is the subject who prefers to learn by himself.</div><div><strong>préfère</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "préférer", which means "to prefer". It indicates a preference or a greater liking for something.</div><div><strong>apprendre</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "apprendre", which means "to learn" in English. It is used here to describe the action that the subject prefers to do.</div><div><strong>seul</strong> This is a French adjective that means "alone" or "by oneself". It signals that the subject prefers to conduct the learning process without assistance.</div>','<h2>Je vais leur enseigner les mathématiques.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it’s used to form the near future tense, indicating an intention or a plan to do something.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun in French, equivalent to "them" in English. It indicates the action is directed toward "them", which in this case refers to an unspecified group of people the speaker will teach.</div><div><strong>enseigner</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to teach" in English. When used with "vais" (from aller), it forms the near future tense, indicating the teaching will happen in the near future.</div><div><strong>les mathématiques</strong> This phrase means "mathematics" in English. In French, subjects like mathematics are often preceded by the definite article "les", even when not used in English.</div>','<h2>Les enfants n’oubliaient pas ce qu’elle leur apprenait.</h2><div><strong>Les</strong> This is the French definite article for plural nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to refer to a specific group of children.</div><div><strong>enfants</strong> This is a French noun meaning "children". It is plural and goes with the plural article "les".</div><div><strong>n’</strong> This is a contraction of "ne", which is a negation particle in French. It is shortened to "n’" because the next word starts with a vowel sound, following French elision rules.</div><div><strong>oubliaient</strong> This is the third-person plural imperfect tense form of the verb "oublier", which means "to forget". In this context, it conveys that the children used not to forget something in the past.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> Together with "ne/n’", it forms the negation "ne... pas" which means "not". It negates the verb "oubliaient".</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "that" or "what" in English, referring to the thing being taught. It introduces a relative clause.</div><div><strong>qu’elle</strong> This is a contraction of "que" (which or that) and "elle" (she), used here to link the relative clause with the main clause. "Qu’elle" means "that she" where "que" serves as a relative pronoun and "elle" is the third-person singular feminine subject pronoun.</div><div><strong>leur</strong> This is an indirect object pronoun meaning "to them". It is used here to indicate to whom she is teaching something.</div><div><strong>apprenait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "apprendre", which means "to teach" or "to learn". In this context, it indicates that she was teaching them something in the past.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);